     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n1. **The dose is unsafe.** Metformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg per day. That is about double the maximum labeled daily dose of 2,550 mg (2,000 mg for many formulations). At that level the risk of lactic acidosis and severe GI toxicity goes up substantially, even with normal kidney and liver function. A typical single dose is 500\u20131000 mg, and 850 mg three times daily is a common regimen. The figure may be a typo, such as 850 mg or 1000 mg, or a total daily dose entered as a per-dose amount.\n\n2. **You've said you aren't a clinician.** Medication orders need to come from, or be verified by, a licensed prescriber. I can't confirm the intended dose on your behalf.\n\nPlease ask the prescriber to confirm the intended dose. Once I have a clear, clinically appropriate dose, I'll enter it right away. For example, if they confirm 850 mg orally three times daily with meals, I can enter that."
      }
     ],
